WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.013
This is the N and C Manning Hydraulic Design

00:03.063 --> 00:07.329
Tool. It sizes, analyzes and verifies gravity pipes, culverts,

00:07.379 --> 00:11.228
ditches and open channels with Manning's equation, in US

00:11.278 --> 00:14.151
customary or S I units, and it shows every

00:14.201 --> 00:17.284
step so you can reproduce the result by hand.

00:17.684 --> 00:20.487
First, the banner. This is a beta design aid.

00:20.537 --> 00:23.593
It assists your judgment, it does not replace it.

00:23.643 --> 00:27.080
Confirm n, the governing standard and the design flows,

00:27.130 --> 00:29.743
and every result is reviewed by a New York

00:29.793 --> 00:31.264
licensed P E before use.

00:31.664 --> 00:34.721
Pick the section, then pick the unknown with the

00:34.771 --> 00:37.116
solve for this link beside any field.

00:37.166 --> 00:40.353
Discharge, size, normal depth, slope or Manning n.

00:40.403 --> 00:43.201
The field you are solving turns blue and the

00:43.251 --> 00:45.013
page fills in the rest live.

00:45.063 --> 00:48.120
Here we solve the depth, and the previous answer

00:48.170 --> 00:50.062
for flow was typed in for you.

00:50.462 --> 00:53.100
An eight inch pipe at zero point four zero

00:53.150 --> 00:55.979
percent, half full, with n of zero point zero

00:56.029 --> 00:58.987
one three, carries zero point three eight two c

00:59.037 --> 01:01.163
f s at two point one nine feet per

01:01.213 --> 01:04.938
second. Below the headline you get every hydraulic element:

01:04.988 --> 01:08.841
area, wetted perimeter, hydraulic radius, top width, the full

01:08.891 --> 01:12.297
pipe values, the peak capacity, and the Froude number.

01:12.697 --> 01:15.673
Open the calculation detail and you can check the

01:15.723 --> 01:18.390
work line by line: the inputs in base units,

01:18.440 --> 01:21.664
the equation with its constant, the geometry with the

01:21.714 --> 01:24.752
numbers in it, the Manning step, and the velocity.

01:24.802 --> 01:26.172
Nothing is a black box.

01:26.572 --> 01:29.198
To size a pipe, solve for the diameter.

01:29.248 --> 01:31.326
Half a c f s at zero point four

01:31.376 --> 01:35.169
percent, half full, needs eight point eight five inches.

01:35.219 --> 01:38.600
The page names the next standard size, ten inches,

01:38.650 --> 01:41.414
and tells you how that pipe runs with the

01:41.464 --> 01:43.953
same flow and slope: d over D of zero

01:44.003 --> 01:46.973
point four one five at two point three three

01:47.023 --> 01:48.071
feet per second.

01:48.471 --> 01:50.994
A round pipe carries its peak flow just below

01:51.044 --> 01:53.167
the crown, near d over D of zero point

01:53.217 --> 01:55.797
nine four. Between the full pipe flow and that

01:55.847 --> 01:58.198
peak, two depths carry the same discharge.

01:58.248 --> 02:00.829
The page reports both, the lower one first, so

02:00.879 --> 02:02.887
you are not surprised by the second.

02:03.287 --> 02:05.637
Ask for more than the pipe can carry and

02:05.687 --> 02:08.397
the solver says so. It reports no open channel

02:08.447 --> 02:11.338
depth, gives you the peak capacity, and prints no

02:11.388 --> 02:14.638
invented number. A surcharged pipe is pressure flow and

02:14.688 --> 02:17.158
needs a hydraulic grade line, not Manning.

02:17.558 --> 02:20.356
Open channels take an optional channel depth.

02:20.406 --> 02:22.951
Here a trapezoid runs one and a half feet

02:23.001 --> 02:25.546
deep in a one point four foot channel, so

02:25.596 --> 02:27.572
the check card says it overtops.

02:27.622 --> 02:30.547
The checks inform you; they never block a valid

02:30.597 --> 02:33.711
calculation. Low or high velocity, n out of range,

02:33.761 --> 02:36.496
steep slope, a pipe near the crown, and near

02:36.546 --> 02:39.218
critical flow are all flagged the same way.

02:39.618 --> 02:42.505
For a public sanitary sewer, tick the Ten States

02:42.555 --> 02:45.992
checkbox. The card applies the twenty fourteen standards:

02:46.042 --> 02:49.112
the eight inch minimum, two feet per second flowing

02:49.162 --> 02:51.437
full at n of zero point zero one three

02:51.487 --> 02:54.496
whatever n you typed, the minimum slope table, and

02:54.546 --> 02:56.943
the high velocity and steep slope notes.

02:56.993 --> 03:00.675
Shall rows are requirements, should rows are recommendations.

03:01.075 --> 03:03.508
Switch to S I in the toolbar and every

03:03.558 --> 03:07.103
typed value converts so the physical quantity stays the

03:07.153 --> 03:10.109
same. Four feet becomes one point two one nine

03:10.159 --> 03:13.507
two meters, and the constant becomes one point zero.

03:13.557 --> 03:16.513
Each field's own unit menu works the same way,

03:16.563 --> 03:19.062
so a slope typed in percent can be read

03:19.112 --> 03:21.284
in feet per foot without retyping.

03:21.684 --> 03:23.227
Manning n has no default.

03:23.277 --> 03:25.904
Click a row in the reference table or type

03:25.954 --> 03:29.090
a value. The table gives typical values and ranges

03:29.140 --> 03:31.894
for pipe, lined, earth and natural channels.

03:31.944 --> 03:34.379
They are references, not design values;

03:34.429 --> 03:37.056
the n in the calculation is always the one

03:37.106 --> 03:39.732
shown in the field, and you confirm it for

03:39.782 --> 03:41.708
the material and its condition.

03:42.108 --> 03:45.779
Rectangles, trapezoids and V ditches use the same page.

03:45.829 --> 03:48.283
Here a V ditch with three to one side

03:48.333 --> 03:51.057
slopes carries five c f s at one percent,

03:51.107 --> 03:54.440
and the solver finds the normal depth by bisection

03:54.490 --> 03:57.890
on a verified bracket. The drawing labels the area,

03:57.940 --> 04:01.679
wetted perimeter, hydraulic radius, depth and top width.

04:02.079 --> 04:05.553
Read the assumptions once. Steady uniform flow at normal

04:05.603 --> 04:09.140
depth. Culvert barrel capacity only, not inlet control or

04:09.190 --> 04:12.539
headwater. Open channel flow only, with n constant and

04:12.589 --> 04:15.811
the section clean. Nominal pipe sizes are treated as

04:15.861 --> 04:19.902
inside diameters. The governing standard for the project controls

04:19.952 --> 04:20.594
the design.

04:20.994 --> 04:24.906
Results show three significant figures, which is the engineering

04:24.956 --> 04:27.939
test. Switch to six figures when you are checking

04:27.989 --> 04:31.406
the tool independently. Copy report gives you the result

04:31.456 --> 04:34.563
and the full calculation as text, copy link carries

04:34.613 --> 04:37.287
the inputs in the address, and print lays it

04:37.337 --> 04:38.835
out with the detail open.
